":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157"],"id":"1984_AMC_12_Q02_m1","label":"Reveal a common factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1984_AMC_12_Q02_m1":["cnm_162","cnm_161","cnm_160","cnm_158"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_162","cnm_161","cnm_160","cnm_158","cnm_156","cnm_157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"1/3","reason":"For x ≠ ±2, (x-2)^2/[(x-2)(x+2)] = (x-2)/(x+2). At x = 4 this is 2/6 = 1/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_158_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_153","cnm_152","cnm_157","cnm_p168"],"id":"m1","label":"Factor, reduce, and substitute","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_158","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_158","cnm_029","cnm_153","cnm_152","cnm_157","cnm_p168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"24","reason":"The denominators are x(x-1) and (x-1)(x+1), so P = x(x-1)(x+1). The fractions become (x+1)/P and x/P, and P(3) = 3·2·4 = 24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_159_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_156","cnm_157"],"id":"m1","label":"Factor, convert, and substitute","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_159","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_159","cnm_029","cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_156","cnm_157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, -27/2","reason":"The relation (2x+2)^2 = x(3x+3) gives (x+1)(x+4) = 0. The value x = -1 does not give a valid nonzero-ratio geometric progression. For x = -4, the terms are -4,-6,-9 with ratio 3/2, so the fourth term is -27/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_151","cnm_156","cnm_157"],"id":"ratio-before-unknown","label":"Find the common ratio first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ratio-before-unknown":["cnm_h207","cnm_158","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_158","cnm_042","cnm_h197","cnm_151","cnm_156","cnm_157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (ax+by)², provided bx+ay ≠ 0","reason":"The numerator is (bx+ay)(a²x²+b²y²)+2abxy(bx+ay) = (bx+ay)(ax+by)². Dividing by bx+ay gives (ax+by)² on the original domain bx+ay ≠ 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"],"id":"common-part-factorization","label":"Collect the shared terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-part-factorization":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: -3/2","reason":"For x≠±1, the fraction equals (x^2-x+1)/(x-1). Its limit as x approaches -1 is 3/(-2)=-3/2, which is the required extension value.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_h027"],"id":"1952_AMC_12_Q36_m1","label":"Cancel the factor causing the missing value","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1952_AMC_12_Q36_m1":["math:elementary-function-limits","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","cnm_158","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["math:elementary-function-limits","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","cnm_158","cnm_029","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_h027"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, no points","reason":"For x ≠ 2, (x² - 4)/(x - 2) = x + 2. Equality with 2x requires x = 2, where the original rational function is undefined. Therefore there is no intersection.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q49","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_152","cnm_157","cnm_042","cnm_194"],"id":"cancel-with-domain","label":"Simplify while retaining the excluded input","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-with-domain":["cnm_165","cnm_195","cnm_158"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_165","cnm_195","cnm_158","cnm_156","cnm_152","cnm_157","cnm_042","cnm_194"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 7","reason":"The quotient equals [(ab+1)/b]/[(ab+1)/a]=a/b, since ab+1 is positive. Thus a=13b and 14b<=100. Exactly b=1,2,...,7 are possible.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_164","cnm_161","cnm_157","cnm_096"],"id":"cancel-the-shared-expression","label":"Cancel the shared expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-the-shared-expression":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_158","cnm_160","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_158","cnm_160","cnm_h020","cnm_164","cnm_161","cnm_157","cnm_096"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_157","status":"mapped","total":45}
